<section xml:id="section-greens-theorem" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Green's Theorem in the Plane</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Green's theorem comes in two forms: one about how much of a vector field
flows <em>across</em> a closed curve, and one about how much flows
<em>along</em> it. Each needs an operation on vector fields of its own,
<term>divergence</term> for the first and <term>curl</term> for the
second. So the section has two halves. Each builds its operation, then
the line integral it goes with, and then the form of Green's theorem that
connects the two when the curve is closed; the Concept Check at the end
uses both.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-green-flux">
<title>Flux and Divergence</title>

<subsubsection xml:id="sec-divergence">
<title>Divergence</title>

<p>
Consider a vector field
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y) = M(x,y)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y)\,\mathbf{j}</m> to be
the velocity field of a fluid. How can we find the net flow rate of the
gas through the boundaries of a rectangle shown in
<xref ref="fig-div-flux"/>?
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-div-flux">
<caption>
The flux of the field <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>
through the four sides of a small rectangle with corner <m>(x,y)</m>,
width <m>\Delta x</m>, and height <m>\Delta y</m>. Each side's outward
arrow is drawn in its own color.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<shortdescription>
A rectangle in a vector field with a differently colored outward arrow through each of its four sides.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-div-flux">
<diagram dimensions="(580,300)" margins="10">
<definition>field(x,y) = (0.4*x + 0.25, 0.32*y + 0.18)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-5.6,-0.6,8.7,4.6)">
<vector-field at="df-field" function="field"
              spacings="((1.8,1.8,5.5),(0.15,1.3,4.1))" scale="0.36"
              stroke="#bbbbbb"/>
<polygon closed="yes" at="df-rect" points="((1.2,1.0),(4.8,1.0),(4.8,3.2),(1.2,3.2))"
         stroke="#2b9ad6" thickness="2.5" fill="none"/>
