</sidebyside>
</figure>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="sec-green-outward-normal">
<title>The Flux Across a Plane Curve</title>

<p>
The first form of Green's theorem, in <xref ref="sec-greens-theorem-forms"/>,
measures how much of a field flows <em>across</em> a closed curve
<m>C</m>: its <term>outward flux</term>
<m>\oint_C \mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{n}\,ds</m>, the line integral of the
component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> along the <term>outward unit normal</term>
<m>\mathbf{n}</m> of the curve. So it uses <m>\mathbf{n}</m> rather than
the unit tangent <m>\mathbf{T}</m> of <xref ref="eq-unit-tangent"/>,
introduced in <xref ref="sec-vector-fields"/>. Suppose <m>C</m> is
traversed counterclockwise and parametrized by
<m>\mathbf{r}(t) = x(t)\,\mathbf{i} + y(t)\,\mathbf{j}</m>. Its velocity,
the tangent vector of <xref ref="eq-tangent-vector"/>, is
<me>
\mathbf{v} = \frac{d\mathbf{r}}{dt}
= \left\langle \frac{dx}{dt},\ \frac{dy}{dt} \right\rangle.
</me>
</p>

<p>
Since <m>\mathbf{n}</m> is a unit normal to the curve, it is
perpendicular to the tangent vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, so
<m>\mathbf{n} \cdot \mathbf{v} = 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
The vector
<m>\left\langle \frac{dy}{dt},\ -\frac{dx}{dt} \right\rangle</m> has
this property, because
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \left\langle \frac{dy}{dt},\ -\frac{dx}{dt} \right\rangle \cdot
\left\langle \frac{dx}{dt},\ \frac{dy}{dt} \right\rangle
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \frac{dy}{dt}\,\frac{dx}{dt} - \frac{dx}{dt}\,\frac{dy}{dt} = 0.
</mrow>
</md>
It is <m>\mathbf{v}</m> turned a quarter turn clockwise. Of the two
directions perpendicular to <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, this is the one that
points out of the region: as <m>C</m> is traversed counterclockwise, the
region <m>R</m> it encloses stays on the left, so turning
<m>\mathbf{v}</m> clockwise makes it point to the right, out of
<m>R</m>. (The opposite vector,
<m>\left\langle -\frac{dy}{dt},\ \frac{dx}{dt} \right\rangle</m>, points
into <m>R</m>.) Therefore the outward normal is
<me>
\left\langle \frac{dy}{dt},\ -\frac{dx}{dt} \right\rangle.
</me>
</p>

<p>
To find the unit normal we divide this vector by its length. Its length
is
<m>\sqrt{\left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^2}
= \left\lVert\mathbf{v}\right\rVert</m>, the same as the length of
<m>\mathbf{v}</m>, so
<men xml:id="eq-outward-unit-normal">
\mathbf{n} = \frac{1}{\left\lVert\mathbf{v}\right\rVert}
\left\langle \frac{dy}{dt},\ -\frac{dx}{dt} \right\rangle.
</men>
Compare it with the unit tangent
<m>\mathbf{T} = \frac{1}{\left\lVert\mathbf{v}\right\rVert}
\left\langle \frac{dx}{dt},\ \frac{dy}{dt} \right\rangle</m> of
<xref ref="eq-unit-tangent"/>: the two have the same components,
swapped, with one sign changed.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-unit-normal-circle">
<title>The Unit Normal to the Unit Circle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the outward unit normal <m>\mathbf{n}</m> and the unit tangent
<m>\mathbf{T}</m> of the unit circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, traversed
counterclockwise.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compare with <xref ref="fig-unit-normal-circle"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
