<subsubsection xml:id="sec-component-test-alternative">
<title>An Alternative Form of the Component Test for Conservative Fields</title>

<p>
Recall the component test for conservative fields:
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="reminder-component-test">
<title>Reminder: Component Test for Conservative Fields</title>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf{F} = M(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{i} + N(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{j}
+ P(x,y,z)\,\mathbf{k}</m> be a field on a connected and simply
connected domain whose component functions have continuous first
partial derivatives. Then <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is conservative if and only
if
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \amp = \frac{\partial N}{\partial z},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial M}{\partial z} \amp = \frac{\partial P}{\partial x},
\quad \text{and}
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-component-test-3d" number="yes">
\frac{\partial N}{\partial x} \amp = \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}.
</mrow>
</md>
</assemblage>

<p>
The three equalities of <xref ref="eq-component-test-3d"/> are
equivalent to
<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = \mathbf{0}</m>, since
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F} \amp =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} \amp \mathbf{j} \amp \mathbf{k} \\
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y} \amp
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
M \amp N \amp P
\end{vmatrix}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial N}{\partial z}
\right) \mathbf{i}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad + \left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial x}
\right) \mathbf{j}
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-curl-cross-product" number="yes">
\amp \quad + \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}
\right) \mathbf{k}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
So to check whether a vector field is conservative or not, we can check
whether its curl is zero or not.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-conservative-green">
<title>Is This Field Conservative?</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the line integral
<m>\displaystyle \oint_C 4x^3 y\, dx + x^4\, dy</m>.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
As you know, the vector form of a line integral is
<m>\displaystyle \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>. Write down
the vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> for the above line integral.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Is this vector field a conservative field? Use the component test,
<xref ref="eq-component-test-3d"/>, to answer.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Using Green's theorem, confirm your answer to part B.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compare with <xref ref="fig-conservative-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5) -- (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {A. $\mathbf F(x,y) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {B.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {C.};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows labelled A, B and C, one for each part.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-conservative-video">
<caption>
The field <m>4x^3 y\,\mathbf{i} + x^4\,\mathbf{j}</m> and its
