<subsection xml:id="subsec-green-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-div-curl-manim"/> puts the two halves of this section side
by side, for fields of its own: a small circle around a point <m>P</m>, the flow of a
fluid across it and along it, and a paddle wheel at <m>P</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-div-curl-manim">
<caption>
Divergence and curl in a moving fluid, animated. The arrows are the
field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the moving dots a fluid carried by it. On the
circle around <m>P</m>, green arrows show fluid leaving and red arrows
fluid entering; blue and orange arrows show the flow along the circle,
with and against the counterclockwise direction. The card divides the
outward flux and the circulation by the area of the circle: shrink the
circle and compare the two ratios with the divergence and the
<m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of the curl you compute at <m>P</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-div-curl-manim"
             iframe="div-curl-manim.html"
             width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
Pick a field from the menu, and click or drag on the plane to move
<m>P</m>; the <q>circle</q> slider shrinks or grows the circle.
<q>Flow</q>, <q>Flux</q>, <q>Circulation</q> and <q>Paddle wheel</q>
switch those layers off and on, <q>Play</q> runs a guided tour, and
<q>Full screen</q> fills the screen with the figure.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-green-circle-video"/> computes both forms of Green's
theorem for the field and the circle of
<xref ref="example-green-circle-normal"/> and
<xref ref="example-green-circle-tangential"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-green-circle-video">
<caption>
Both forms of Green's theorem for
<m>\mathbf{F} = (x-y)\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m> on the unit
circle, computed side by side. Use it to check your values of the
outward flux and the counterclockwise circulation.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-green-circle"
       source="videos/green_unit_circle_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

